From 83057cb546335ce3846515ae909108c64f7f37c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Dawid=20Wr=C3=B3bel?= <me@dawidwrobel.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 02:18:43 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/41] Documentation: tee: document the QSEECOM driver

Describe the interface: sessions opened by application name, the four-parameter
invoke with its address-patch descriptor, application loading and the unload on
session close, and the listener services with their single per-device
supplicant queue.

Record the security position too, since it is not obvious from the code. The
driver cannot validate what a command means -- it does not know any
application's payload format -- so anyone able to open the client device can
send arbitrary commands to any loaded trusted application. This interface has a
history: trustlet command handlers reachable this way (CVE-2022-48334), the
communication path used to reach arbitrary kernel read/write (CVE-2021-1961),
the downstream driver's own use-after-free and race (CVE-2019-14040,
CVE-2019-14041), and unprivileged access to the character device as an
escalation step (CVE-2018-9411).

Device node permissions are therefore part of the security boundary rather than
a packaging detail, and the Kconfig help says so where an integrator will see
it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
